class:: ModalPlate
summary:: Moving-excitation and pickup physical rectangular modal plate
categories:: UGens>Reverbs, Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN, Guides/CamaraObscura

description::

image::../Guides/ir_plate.png#ModalPlate shows the high-frequency sinusoidal beating characteristic of a ringing physical structure. You can clearly see the interference of specific plate eigenmodes rather than broadband noise.::

Conceptually, modal synthesis creates reverberation by simulating the physical vibrations of a two-dimensional object—like a sheet of metal or glass—when struck. Unlike algorithmic reverbs that model air in a room, plate reverbs mimic the dense, shimmering, and instantly-dispersing mechanical waves of a physical plate, which has historically been highly prized in studios for thickening vocals and drums.

Technically, `ModalPlate` solves the Kirchhoff–Love plate equations for a rectangular boundary, breaking the physical vibration down into independent resonant modes. It then excites and reads from these modes using physical coordinates. By dynamically interpolating the physical mode shapes at block boundaries, the UGen allows both the excitation source and the pickup microphones to smoothly move across the surface of the plate in real-time, capturing realistic phase interactions and modal cancellations.

ModalPlate renders every mode derived by a
link::Classes/ModalPlateModel::. Once per server block it evaluates the
simply-supported sin-sin mode shapes at normalized excitation and pickup
positions. Residues move toward those targets with a 20 ms time constant,
traversing nodal lines without audio-rate trigonometry or clicks.

Output is one wet audio channel. Mode count is fixed by the model and can be
as high as 4096; CPU is linear and equivalent to a modal bank of that size
plus bounded block-rate residue updates. Pickup residues include
mode-count normalization for practical headroom.

code::dampingScale:: changes every modal T60 while keeping radii stable.
code::drive:: saturates excitation only and is an artistic control, not a
claim of nonlinear tension. Reset clears modal state. This public API has no
freeze because the implementation specification reserves only reset for the
plate renderer.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/Klank::. ModalPlate differs by
deriving frequencies from physical plate parameters and by continuously
moving excitation/pickup participation through physical mode shapes.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: input
Mono audio input.

argument:: modelBuf
Loaded link::Classes/ModalPlateModel::.

argument:: excitationX
Normalized excitation x position, clipped to 0 through 1.

argument:: excitationY
Normalized excitation y position, clipped to 0 through 1.

argument:: pickupX
Normalized pickup x position, clipped to 0 through 1.

argument:: pickupY
Normalized pickup y position, clipped to 0 through 1.

argument:: pitch
Artistic frequency multiplier from 1/32 through 16, Nyquist-clamped.

argument:: dampingScale
Modal T60 multiplier from 0.02 through 50.

argument:: drive
Bounded excitation saturation from 0 through 16.

argument:: reset
Rising edge clears all modal states.

returns::
One wet audio signal.

method:: kr
Unavailable; audio-rate only.

examples::

The following examples have been structured into individual blocks so you can easily run them step-by-step.

code::
// Boot the server first!
s.boot;

// 1. Prepare the model and source material
(
// Use a fallback to ExampleFiles.child if the external sample bundle is not installed
~path = Platform.userExtensionDir +/+ "CamaraObscura/sounds/";
~load = { |name| if(File.exists(~path +/+ name)) { ~path +/+ name } { ExampleFiles.child } };

~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("vocal.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum1.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum2.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("guitar.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("violin.wav"), channels: [0]);

~model = ModalPlateModel.rectangular(1.0, 1.5, 0.002, 7850, 2e11, 0.3, 0.01, 512, \simplySupported, s.sampleRate).load(s);
)

// 2. Play the reverb (wait a moment for the buffers to load before executing this)
(
x = {
    var input = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~source, BufRateScale.kr(~source), loop: 1);
    // var input = SoundIn.ar(0); // alternative live input
    
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.12]));
    var wet = ModalPlate.ar(input, ~model, [0.3, 0.3], [0.7, 0.7])[0];
    
    (input + wet) * fade * 0.25 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
x.free;
~source.free;
~model.free;
::

// Creative Example: Scanning the plate with a moving pickup
code::
// 1. Load the model
~creativeModel = ~creativeModel = ModalPlateModel.rectangular(2.0, 2.0, 0.005, 7850, 2e11, 0.3, 0.005, 512, \simplySupported, s.sampleRate).load(s);

// 2. Play
(
y = {
    var pickupX = LFDNoise3.kr(0.5).range(0.1, 0.9);
    var pickupY = LFDNoise3.kr(0.4).range(0.1, 0.9);
    ModalPlate.ar(Dust.ar(10), ~creativeModel, [0.5, 0.5], [pickupX, pickupY])[0]
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
y.free;
~creativeModel.free;
::
